---
/**
 * website/src/pages/changelog.astro — the changelog (website plan, W1.9).
 *
 * The page is an index of `docs/releases/vX.Y.Z.md`: the hand-written Highlights of every
 * release, newest first, rendered from the files themselves. Three things follow from that
 * and are worth stating here rather than in the component:
 *
 *   1. **The entries are built during the build, and nothing is committed.** W0.6's
 *      `src/lib/changelog.mjs` reads the release files and this page renders what it
 *      returns, so a Highlights file that lands on `main` before its tag exists is on the
 *      page at the next build and there is no generated copy in the tree to go stale
 *      (D100). The page is also the first reader-facing index of those files: `README.md`
 *      deliberately keeps no changelog section and points at the Releases page instead
 *      (finding 44).
 *   2. **The page writes nothing of its own.** Its heading names it, the component renders
 *      each release's end-user summary from `website/data/release-summaries.yaml` and then
 *      the release file's own lines below it, and `website/test/changelog-page.mjs` fails on
 *      a sentence that is in neither: the page-level form of W0.6's file-level check. Both
 *      halves of an entry are text a file of this repository carries, which is what lets
 *      them exist at all.
 *   3. **The repository the entries link is a fact.** It comes from `website/data/site.yaml`
 *      through the content pipeline (W0.2, W1.3), so the release URLs are built from the
 *      site's own fact and no host is typed into a template.
 *
 * Nothing here fetches anything: the build is network-free and reads this repository only
 * (Global Constraints 4 and 5).
 *
 * **The page's own look is the bold one, and it is the owner's brief of 2026-10-21** — the
 * same look the owner approved on the home page (`website/design-ref/HomeV3.dc.html`,
 * `src/pages/index.astro`) applied to this page, which has no board of its own. So the page
 * opens with the display scale's largest step: `Changelog` at the weight, the tracking and the
 * leading the boards set a headline in (`base.css`'s `.display-xl`), its word in the accent's
 * gradient (`.gradient-text`, the same class the hero's last two words are drawn in), with the
 * room under it a page's first block gets. **The page adds no words to do it**: the headline is
 * still the page's one `h1` and the only text above the first release, which is why the page
 * has no eyebrow, no pill and no lead paragraph — every sentence on this page is a release
 * file's or a release summary's, and `website/test/changelog-page.mjs` refuses one that is
 * neither. The entries under it are the cards `Changelog.astro` draws.
 */
import path from 'node:path'
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Changelog from '../components/Changelog.astro'
import { buildChangelog } from '../lib/changelog.mjs'
import { loadReleaseSummaries, loadSite, REPO_ROOT } from '../content.ts'

// The releases, read from docs/releases/ at build time (W0.6): newest first, each entry
// the file's own tag, bullets and operator steps, and its `released` flag from the plugin's
// own version (the owner's brief of 2026-10-19) — never from the checkout's tags, which a
// deploy build does not carry.
//
// **Why the two files are passed rather than defaulted.** The generator finds the repository
// by walking up from its own file, which is right when it is loaded from `website/src/lib/`
// — as W0.6's own test loads it — and wrong inside a build: Astro bundles it into a chunk
// under `website/dist/.prerender/`, where that walk lands on `website/` and the release
// directory it looks for is `website/docs/releases/`, which does not exist.
// `website/src/content.ts` solves exactly this problem for the site's facts
// (`findSiteRoot`, W1.3) and exports the repository root it found, so the page passes the
// directory of release files and the plugin manifest the generator should read, and the
// generator itself is left as W0.6 wrote it.
const releasesDir = path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'docs', 'releases')
const pluginFile = path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'plugin', '.claude-plugin', 'plugin.json')
const entries = buildChangelog({ releasesDir, pluginFile })

// The end-user summaries below each release's heading: the per-release lines the owner's
// brief of 2026-10-19 asked for, read from the site's own data through the content pipeline
// (W1.3) so the page writes no prose of its own. The component renders the summary it finds
// and stops the build when a release has none.
const summaries = loadReleaseSummaries()

// The site's facts, through the same pipeline every page renders from: the repository is
// the one fact this page needs, and `loadSite()` fails the build if the facts are not
// there rather than rendering a page with a hole in it (W1.3).
const repository = loadSite().facts.repository
---

<Base title="Changelog">
  {/* The page's head: the headline, and the room under it. `header` inside `main` is not a
      landmark (a banner is a `header` of the body, and `website/test/a11y.mjs` counts the
      page-level ones), so it is a box and nothing more. */}
  <header class="changelog-head">
    {/* The boards' background: the grid and the two soft glows behind the top of a page,
        drawn by `base.css`'s `.hero-glow` — the class the home page's hero opens with, used
        here rather than a second drawing of the same thing. It is decoration and says so
        (`aria-hidden`), and it paints no text. */}
    <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <h1 class="changelog-title display-xl">
      <span class="gradient-text">Changelog</span>
    </h1>
  </header>
  <Changelog entries={entries} summaries={summaries} repository={repository} />
</Base>

<style>
  /* The page's head, in the page's own stylesheet: the headline, the room under it and the
     boards' background behind it. The room above it is `main`'s own padding, which every page
     of the site gets, and the gutter is `main`'s too — this block adds only the step between
     the headline and the first card, and it is a step of the spacing scale rather than a
     number. `position: relative` and `overflow: hidden` are what the glow layer needs
     (`base.css`, section 2c). */
  .changelog-head {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-2xl);
  }

  /* The headline paints above the glow: a positioned box paints after a static one, and the
     glow is the head's first child (the hero's copy is positioned for the same reason). */
  .changelog-title {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0;
    /* The headline is one word, and the display scale's balance (`text-wrap: balance`) has
       nothing to balance: it is set on one line at every width it fits, which the clamp in
       `.display-xl` is what decides. */
    max-width: 20ch;
  }
</style>
